  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-95139",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the time a full charge lasts. Then we have $X∼N(26,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>30).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<20)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<20) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-26}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-6}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{-6}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.14027 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.14027} \\\\  & ≈7.1291.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(30<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>30) & =P(Z>\\frac{30-26}{7.1291}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.56) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.56) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.56) \\\\  & =1-0.7123 \\\\  & ≈28.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $28.8%$ of the times the battery lasts more than $30$ hours.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
